Why Ephemeral Storage Fails

Containers have an ephemeral root filesystem. If a pod running a database crashes or gets rescheduled to another node, all writes stored inside the container disappear.

To solve this, Kubernetes decouples storage into three layers:

StorageClass (SC): The storage backend blueprint (e.g., AWS EBS, GCP PD, or local hostpath in Minikube) that enables dynamic provisioning.

PersistentVolume (PV): An actual piece of allocated storage in the cluster.

PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC): A request/ticket submitted by an application declaring how much storage and what access mode it needs.


+------------------------------------+
       |            StorageClass            | (Provisioner Engine)
       +-----------------+------------------+
                         |
                         v (Dynamically creates)
+---------------------------------------------------+
|               PersistentVolume (PV)               | (Cluster-wide resource)
|               Capacity: 1Gi, ReadWriteOnce        |
+------------------------+--------------------------+
                         |
                         | Bound to
                         v
+---------------------------------------------------+
|            PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)            | (Namespace-scoped request)
|            Requests: 1Gi                          |
+------------------------+--------------------------+
                         |
                         | Mounted as volume
                         v
+---------------------------------------------------+
|                     Pod Spec                      |
|            volumeMounts: /usr/share/nginx/html    |
+---------------------------------------------------+

Step 1: Check the Default StorageClass in Minikube

Minikube comes with a built-in dynamic provisioner out of the box:

kubectl get storageclass

You will see standard (default) using the k8s.io/minikube-hostpath provisioner.

Step 2: Create a PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)

Save this manifest as storage-demo.yaml:

cat <<EOF > storage-demo.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: web-data-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 500Mi
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: persistent-web
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: persistent-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: persistent-web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:alpine
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
        volumeMounts:
        - name: html-storage
          mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html
      volumes:
      - name: html-storage
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: web-data-pvc
EOF

kubectl apply -f storage-demo.yaml